## Test that BOLT re-relocates the PPC64 ELFv2 global entry point (GEP)
## TOC-recompute preamble instead of copying it verbatim.
##
## Every global function that touches the TOC starts with
##
##   addis r2, r12, (.TOC. - func)@ha    R_PPC64_REL16_HA .TOC. + 0
##   addi  r2, r2,  (.TOC. - func)@l     R_PPC64_REL16_LO .TOC. + 4
##
## Both immediates are relative to the address of the instruction holding
## them, so they are only valid at the address the static linker chose. BOLT
## used to drop these relocations (R_PPC64_REL16* were missing from
## isSupportedPPC64) and emit the immediates unchanged at the function's new
## address, so r2 ended up off by the distance the function moved and the
## first TOC access after the preamble read unmapped memory. The original
## symptom was a SIGSEGV inside an IFUNC resolver called from
## _dl_relocate_object (bolt/test/runtime/iplt.c).
##
## The preamble only runs when a function is entered at its global entry
## point, so the test calls through a volatile function pointer; a direct
## call would be retargeted to the local entry point and skip it.
# REQUIRES: system-linux
## -mcpu=power8 is what keeps Power10 prefixed/pcrel instructions out; do not
## add -mno-prefixed/-mno-pcrel, which the system gcc that lit runs with on
## AlmaLinux 8 (8.5.0) does not accept.
# RUN: gcc -O2 -mcpu=power8 -fno-pic -no-pie -Wl,--emit-relocs \
# RUN:     -fno-optimize-sibling-calls -mtraceback=no \
# RUN:     %S/Inputs/gep-toc-recompute.c -o %t.exe

## Guard against the input silently losing the relocations under test, which
## would make the run below pass for the wrong reason.
# RUN: llvm-readelf -rW %t.exe | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=INPUT
# INPUT-DAG: R_PPC64_REL16_HA
# INPUT-DAG: R_PPC64_REL16_LO

# RUN: llvm-bolt %t.exe -o %t.bolt 2>&1 | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=BOLT
# BOLT: BOLT-INFO: Target architecture: powerpc64le
# BOLT: BOLT-INFO: enabling relocation mode

## The rewritten binary must still compute the right TOC base: it prints the
## value it loaded through r2 and exits non-zero if it is wrong.
# RUN: %t.bolt | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=EXEC
# EXEC: value = 33
